A||||5||gtsonly||Rebuilding the 2.8, need help!!! In car ring job!!!||||5||
Z||000000||gtsonly||08-20-2001||10:41 AM||||I have a 2.8 thats burning oil something bad. Only 83,000 miles on the engine. I did a compression and cyl's 1 through 5 were 115 and 6 pumped up to 120 but leaked down to 95 where it held. Rings or Valves????? Thats the question. I am doing this rebuild in my garage. Has anyone done a in car ring job? If so can you give me some advise. I hope it to be the valves but you never know with these cars. <P>------------------<BR>Chris Hatch <BR>1986 GT w/T-TOPs||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||08-20-2001||11:23 AM||||Are your compression #s dry or wet?<BR>If they are dry, try squirting some oil in the cylinders and take another reading on all. This check will see if the problem is in the rings or the valves.<BR>Replacing rings in the car with the exception of a 4 banger with good cylinders, is not recommended as the cylinders need to be honed to assist new rings to seat.<BR>At start, do you see any smoke from the exhaust? If so, how much?||||reg||1
Z||000002||gtsonly||08-20-2001||12:15 PM||||When I did the compression it was dry. Seeing the engine is already partly torn down, it will be hard to obtain a wet reading. There was the puff of smoke on start up(valves) but also under acceleration did the beast smoke like a hooker on a slow saturday nite. Any thoughts? <P>------------------<BR>Chris Hatch <BR>1986 GT w/T-TOPs||||reg||3
Z||000003||EdsB52||08-20-2001||12:17 PM||||It's generally possible to hone the cyliders in most cars, but not the Fiero 6 cyl.  I would suggest pulling the motor.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||08-20-2001||12:20 PM||||If you've got compressed air, you can do a leak-down test to confirm whether it's leaking from the rings or the valves, I think.<BR>-----------------------<BR>Added:<BR>...even with the manifold off.  Turn engine to get a cylinders on its compression stroke (or, better yet, remove all the pushrods)  apply compressed air to the spark plug hole, and listen at the manifolds (or ports) to see if air leaks out the intake port or exhaust port.  If it leaks out mostly to the crankcase, then it'll need rings.  And be careful, the engine may rotate when you apply the air to the cylinders.     <P><B>Think</B> that's how it's supposed to work  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.  I bet people will tell you "don't waste your time - just do a ring and valve job," but I've done goofy things to get by, myself.  You sound like me (no insult intended, BTW  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ).    <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 08-20-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000005||gtsonly||08-20-2001||12:25 PM||||Even with the manifold off? I got the engine to the point of taking the heads off. Are there any tattle tale signs to look for?<P>------------------<BR>Chris Hatch <BR>1986 GT w/T-TOPs||||reg||5
